1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:11,800
♪ 'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves ♪
♪ Did gyre and gimble in the wabe; ♪

2
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:23,100
♪ All mimsy were the borogoves, ♪
♪ And the mome raths outgrabe. ♪

3
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:34,200
♪ "Beware the Jabberwock, my son! ♪
♪ The jaws that bite, the claws that catch! ♪

4
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:45,400
♪ Beware the Jubjub bird, and shun ♪
♪ The frumious Bandersnatch!" ♪

5
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:56,500
♪ He took his vorpal sword in hand: ♪
♪ Long time the manxome foe he sought— ♪

6
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:07,900
♪ So rested he by the Tumtum tree, ♪
♪ And stood awhile in thought. ♪

7
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:19,000
♪ And as in uffish thought he stood, ♪
♪ The Jabberwock, with eyes of flame, ♪

8
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:30,200
♪ Came whiffling through the tulgey wood, ♪
♪ And burbled as it came! ♪

9
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:41,500
♪ One, two! One, two! And through and through ♪
♪ The vorpal blade went snicker-snack! ♪

10
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:58,500
♪ He left it dead, and with its head ♪
♪ He went galumphing back. ♪

11
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,900
(Alice) A mad tea-party...

12
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:08,200
There was a table set out under a tree in front of the house.

13
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,400
The March Hare and the Hatter were having tea at it.

14
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,700
A Dormouse was sitting between them, fast asleep.

15
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:21,500
The other two were using it as a cushion, resting their elbows on it.

16
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:25,900
They were talking right over its head.

17
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,800
*Very uncomfortable for the Dormouse...*

18
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:32,900
Only, as it's asleep, I suppose it doesn't mind.

19
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,800
The table was a large one, but the three were all crowded together at one corner of it.

20
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:39,500
No room! No room!

21
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,500
There's plenty of room!

22
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:46,400
She sat down in a large arm-chair at one end of the table.

23
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:48,900
Have some wine.

24
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:51,900
I don't see any wine.

25
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:54,200
There isn't any.

26
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:57,900
Then it wasn't very civil of you to offer it.

27
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:01,800
It wasn't very civil of you to sit down without being invited.

28
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:05,900
I didn't know it was your table; it's laid for a great many more than three.

29
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:08,700
Your hair wants cutting.

30
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:12,800
You should learn not to make personal remarks; it's very rude.

31
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,200
Why is a raven like a writing-desk?

32
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:19,500
Come, we shall have some fun now!

33
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:22,900
I'm glad they've begun asking riddles.

34
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:25,800
I believe I can guess that.

35
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:29,700
Do you mean that you think you can find out the answer to it?

36
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:31,900
Exactly so.

37
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:34,800
Then you should say what you mean.

38
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:38,900
I do; at least—at least I mean what I say—that's the same thing, you know.

39
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:41,800
Not the same thing a bit!

40
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:46,900
You might just as well say that "I see what I eat" is the same thing as "I eat what I see"!

41
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:51,900
You might just as well say that "I like what I get" is the same thing as "I get what I like"!

42
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:56,900
You might just as well say that "I breathe when I sleep" is the same thing as "I sleep when I breathe"!

43
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,800
It IS the same thing with you.

44
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:04,500
(shuffling tea cups) The conversation here dropped, and the party sat silent for a minute.

45
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:09,800
Alice thought over all she could remember about ravens and writing-desks, which wasn't much.

46
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:13,800
The Hatter was the first to break the silence.

47
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:16,900
What day of the month is it?

48
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:21,500
He had taken his watch out of his pocket, and was looking at it uneasily.

49
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,900
He shook it every now and then, and put it to his ear.

50
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:28,200
The fourth.

51
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,800
Two days wrong!

52
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:33,500
(sighing) I told you butter wouldn't suit the works!

53
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,200
It was the BEST butter.

54
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:40,200
Yes, but some crumbs must have got in as well.

55
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:43,900
You shouldn't have put it in with the bread-knife.

56
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:47,900
The March Hare took the watch and looked at it gloomily.

57
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:51,900
He dipped it into his cup of tea, and looked at it again.

58
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:54,800
But he could think of nothing better to say than his first remark.

59
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:57,500
It was the BEST butter, you know.

60
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:01,800
Alice had been looking over his shoulder with some curiosity.

61
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,900
What a funny watch!

62
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:08,900
It tells the day of the month, and doesn't tell what o'clock it is!

63
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,500
Why should it?

64
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:14,800
Does your watch tell you what year it is?

65
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:16,900
Of course not.

66
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:20,900
But that's because it stays the same year for such a long time together.

67
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:23,800
Which is just the case with mine.

68
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:27,500
Alice felt dreadfully puzzled.

69
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:31,900
The Hatter's remark seemed to have no sort of meaning in it, and yet it was certainly English.

70
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:34,900
I don't quite understand you.

71
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,800
The Dormouse is asleep again.

72
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:41,200
He poured a little hot tea upon its nose.

73
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:45,900
The Dormouse shook its head impatiently, and said, without opening its eyes...

74
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:50,900
Of course, of course; just what I was going to remark myself.

75
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:53,900
Have you guessed the riddle yet?

76
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:56,500
No, I give it up.

77
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:58,800
What's the answer?

78
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:01,500
I haven't the slightest idea.

79
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:03,800
Nor I.

80
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:08,200
Alice sighed wearily.

81
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:12,900
I think you might do something better with the time than waste it in asking riddles that have no answers.

82
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:17,900
If you knew Time as well as I do, you wouldn't talk about wasting IT. It's HIM.

83
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,500
I don't know what you mean.

84
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:24,200
Of course you don't!

85
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,500
I dare say you never even spoke to Time!

86
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:31,900
Perhaps not, but I know I have to beat time when I learn music.

87
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,200
Ah! That accounts for it.

88
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:36,500
He won't stand beating.

89
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:40,900
Now, if you only kept on good terms with him, he'd do almost anything you liked with the clock.

90
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:44,900
For instance, suppose it were nine o'clock in the morning, just time to begin lessons...

91
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:48,900
You'd only have to whisper a hint to Time, and round goes the clock in a twinkling!

92
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:51,500
Half-past one, time for dinner!

93
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:54,500
(whispering) I only wish it was.

94
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:58,500
That would be grand, certainly, but then—I shouldn't be hungry for it, you know.

95
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:02,500
Not at first, perhaps, but you could keep it to half-past one as long as you liked.

96
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:05,200
Is that the way you manage?

97
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,200
The Hatter shook his head mournfully.

98
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:13,900
Not I! We quarrelled last March—just before HE went mad, you know—

99
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:16,900
(pointing with his teaspoon at the March Hare)

100
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:20,900
It was at the great concert given by the Queen of Hearts.

101
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:24,200
And I had to sing...

102
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:28,400
"Twinkle, twinkle, little bat! How I wonder what you're at!"

103
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,400
You know the song, perhaps?

104
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:33,900
I've heard something like it.

105
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:37,200
It goes on, you know, in this way...

106
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:41,200
"Up above the world you fly, like a tea-tray in the sky."

107
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:43,400
"Twinkle, twinkle—"

108
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,500
Here the Dormouse shook itself, and began singing in its sleep...

109
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:54,900
♪ Twinkle, twinkle, twinkle, twinkle... ♪

110
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:59,200
And went on so long that they had to pinch it to make it stop.

111
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:03,400
Well, I'd hardly finished the first verse, when the Queen bawled out...

112
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,500
"He's murdering the time! Off with his head!"

113
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:08,900
How dreadfully savage!

114
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:12,900
And ever since then, he won't do a thing I ask!

115
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:15,500
It's always six o'clock now.

116
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:18,500
Is that the reason so many tea-things are put out here?

117
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:22,800
Yes, that's it. It's always tea-time.

118
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:26,900
And we've no time to wash the things between whiles.

119
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:29,900
Then you keep moving round, I suppose?

120
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:31,900
Exactly so, as the things get used up.

121
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:35,400
But what happens when you come to the beginning again?

122
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,400
Suppose we change the subject. I'm getting tired of this.

123
00:08:39,900 --> 00:08:42,500
I vote the young lady tells us a story.

124
00:08:43,100 --> 00:08:45,400
I'm afraid I don't know one.

125
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,500
Then the Dormouse shall! Wake up, Dormouse!

126
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:52,900
They pinched it on both sides at once.

127
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:55,900
I wasn't asleep.

128
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:59,900
I heard every word you fellows were saying.

129
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:02,200
Tell us a story!

130
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:04,500
Yes, please do!

131
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:07,500
And be quick about it, or you'll be asleep again before it's done.

132
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:11,400
Once upon a time there were three little sisters...

133
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,400
And their names were Elsie, Lacie, and Tillie...

134
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,800
And they lived at the bottom of a well—

135
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:20,500
What did they live on?

136
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:24,200
Alice always took a great interest in questions of eating and drinking.

137
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:26,900
They lived on treacle.

138
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:29,900
They couldn't have done that, you know. It would have made them ill.

139
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:32,200
So they were. Very ill.

140
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:35,900
Alice tried to fancy to herself what such an extraordinary way of living would be like...

141
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:38,500
But it puzzled her too much.

142
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:41,900
But why did they live at the bottom of a well?

143
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:44,800
Take some more tea.

144
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:47,900
I've had nothing yet, so I can't take more.

145
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:51,500
You mean you can't take LESS.

146
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:55,200
It's very easy to take MORE than nothing.

147
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:58,200
Nobody asked YOUR opinion.

148
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:00,500
Who's making personal remarks now?

149
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,800
The Hatter hungrily eyed the remaining cake.

150
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:06,900
Alice did not quite know what to say to this.

151
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:10,200
So she helped herself to some tea and bread-and-butter.

152
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,900
Then she turned to the Dormouse, and repeated her question.

153
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:17,200
Why did they live at the bottom of a well?

154
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:20,900
The Dormouse again took a minute or two to think about it.

155
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:23,800
It was a treacle-well.

156
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:26,500
There's no such thing!

157
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:29,900
The Hatter and the March Hare went "Shsh! shsh!"

158
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:33,800
The Dormouse sulkily remarked...

159
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:37,500
If you can't be civil, you'd better finish the story for yourself.

160
00:10:38,100 --> 00:10:40,200
No, please go on!

161
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:42,900
I won't interrupt you any more.

162
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:45,900
There may be ONE treacle-well.

163
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:48,200
One, indeed!

164
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:51,500
And so they were learning to draw...

165
00:10:52,100 --> 00:10:55,200
The Dormouse went on, yawning and rubbing its eyes.

166
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:57,900
It was getting very sleepy.

167
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:01,200
And they drew all manner of things...

168
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:04,500
Everything that begins with an M.

169
00:11:05,100 --> 00:11:06,800
Why with an M?

170
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:09,200
Why not?

171
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:11,500
Alice was silent.

172
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:15,400
The Dormouse had closed its eyes by this time.

173
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,900
It was fast asleep once more.

174
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,800
But on being pinched by the Hatter, it woke up with a little shriek.

175
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:25,900
...that begins with an M, such as mouse-traps...

176
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:29,400
And the moon, and memory, and muchness...

177
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,900
You know you say things are "much of a muchness"—

178
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:37,800
Did you ever see such a thing as a drawing of a muchness?

179
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:41,200
Really, now you ask me, I don't think—

180
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:43,900
Then you shouldn't talk.

181
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:47,200
This piece of rudeness was more than Alice could bear.

182
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:51,200
She got up in great disgust, and walked off.

183
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:54,900
The Dormouse fell asleep instantly.

184
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:59,200
Neither of the others took the least notice of her going.

185
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:03,400
Though she looked back once or twice, half hoping that they would call after her.

186
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:07,500
The last time she saw them, they were trying to put the Dormouse into the teapot.

187
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:10,800
At any rate I'll never go THERE again!

188
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:14,200
It's the stupidest tea-party I ever was at in all my life!

189
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:17,900
As she said this, she noticed a tree with a door leading right into it.

190
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:20,500
That's very curious!

191
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:23,500
But everything's curious today.

192
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:25,900
I think I may as well go in at once.

193
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:28,200
And in she went.

194
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:31,900
Once more she found herself in the long hall.

195
00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:35,200
And close to the little glass table.

196
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:38,900
Now, I'll manage better this time.

197
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:42,800
She began by taking the little golden key, and unlocking the door that led into the garden.

198
00:12:43,400 --> 00:12:46,900
Then she set to work nibbling at the mushroom she kept in her pocket.

199
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:49,500
Until she was about a foot high.

200
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:53,400
Then she walked down the little passage.

201
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,800
And THEN she found herself at last in the beautiful garden.

202
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:01,200
Among the bright flower-beds and the cool fountains.

203
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:05,200
A large rose-tree stood near the entrance of the garden.

204
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:08,900
The roses growing on it were white, but there were three gardeners at it.

205
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:12,400
Busily painting them red.

206
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,500
Alice thought this a very curious thing.

207
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:18,900
She went nearer to watch them.

208
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:22,800
Just as she came up to them she heard one of them say...

209
00:13:23,400 --> 00:13:25,900
Look out now, Five! Don't go splashing paint over me like that!

210
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,200
I couldn't help it. Seven flung my elbow.

211
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:32,900
That's right, Seven! Always lay the blame on others!

212
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:35,500
YOU'D better not talk!

213
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:39,400
I heard the Queen say only yesterday you deserved to be beheaded!

214
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,800
What for?

215
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:44,500
That's none of YOUR business, Two!

216
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:47,900
Yes, it IS his business! And I'll tell him!

217
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:51,400
It was for bringing the cook tulip-roots instead of onions.

218
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,800
Seven flung his brush down.

219
00:14:00,100 --> 00:14:02,900
Well, of all the unjust things—

220
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:06,800
He began, but his eye chanced to fall upon Alice, as she stood watching them.

221
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:09,900
He checked himself suddenly.

222
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:12,800
The others looked round also.

223
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:15,500
They all bowed low.

224
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:18,900
Would you tell me, please, why you are painting those roses?

225
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:21,500
Two began, in a low voice.

226
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:24,900
Why, the fact is, you see, Miss...

227
00:14:25,500 --> 00:14:28,800
This here rose-tree ought to have been a RED rose-tree.

228
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:32,900
And we put a white one in by mistake.

229
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:36,900
And if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off, you know.

230
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:40,200
So you see, Miss, we're doing our best, afore she comes, to—

231
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:43,400
At this moment Five, who had been anxiously looking across the garden, called out...

232
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:45,900
The Queen! The Queen!

233
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:49,800
The three gardeners instantly threw themselves flat upon their faces.

234
00:14:50,400 --> 00:14:53,500
There was a sound of many footsteps.

235
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:56,900
Alice looked round, eager to see the Queen.

236
00:14:57,500 --> 00:15:00,800
First came ten soldiers carrying clubs.

237
00:15:01,400 --> 00:15:04,900
These were all shaped like the three gardeners, oblong and flat.

238
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:08,200
With their hands and feet at the corners.

239
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:11,500
Next the ten courtiers.

240
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:15,500
These were ornamented all over with diamonds.

241
00:15:16,100 --> 00:15:18,900
And walked two and two, as the soldiers did.

242
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:22,800
After these came the royal children.

243
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:26,900
There were ten of them, and the little dears came jumping merrily along hand in hand.

244
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:29,200
They were ornamented with hearts.

245
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:32,900
Next came the guests, mostly Kings and Queens.

246
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:36,500
And among them the White Rabbit.

247
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:40,200
He was talking in a hurried nervous manner.

248
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:43,500
Smiling at everything that was said.

249
00:15:44,100 --> 00:15:46,900
He went by without noticing her.

250
00:15:47,500 --> 00:15:50,400
Then followed the Knave of Hearts, bearing the King's crown on a crimson velvet cushion.

251
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,200
And, last of all this grand procession, came THE KING AND QUEEN OF HEARTS.

252
00:15:55,800 --> 00:15:58,900
Alice was rather doubtful whether she ought not to lie down on her face like the three gardeners.

253
00:15:59,400 --> 00:16:01,800
But she could not remember ever having heard of such a rule.

254
00:16:02,300 --> 00:16:04,900
And besides, what would be the use of a procession...

255
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:08,200
If people all had to lie down upon their faces, so that they couldn't see it?

256
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:11,500
So she stood still where she was, and waited.

257
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:15,200
When the procession came opposite to Alice, they all stopped and looked at her.

258
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:17,800
The Queen said severely...

259
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:20,200
Who is this?

260
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:23,500
She said it to the Knave of Hearts, who only bowed and smiled in reply.

261
00:16:24,100 --> 00:16:25,900
Idiot!

262
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:28,900
Tossing her head impatiently, she turned to Alice.

263
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:31,500
What is your name, child?

264
00:16:32,100 --> 00:16:34,200
My name is Alice, so please your Majesty.

265
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:37,900
She said it very civilly, but she added to herself...

266
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:41,200
*Why, they're only a pack of cards, after all. I needn't be afraid of them!*

267
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:44,200
And who are THESE?

268
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:47,900
Pointing to the three gardeners who were lying round the rose-tree.

269
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:51,800
For, you see, as they were lying on their faces, and the pattern on their backs was the same...

270
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:55,200
She could not tell whether they were gardeners, or soldiers, or courtiers.

271
00:16:55,800 --> 00:16:57,900
How should I know?

272
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:00,900
It's no business of MINE.

273
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:04,900
The Queen turned crimson with fury, and, after glaring at her for a moment like a wild beast, screamed...

274
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:07,500
Off with her head! Off—

275
00:17:08,100 --> 00:17:09,800
Nonsense!

276
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:12,900
Alice said very loudly and decidedly, and the Queen was silent.

277
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:16,200
The King laid his hand upon her arm, and timidly said...

278
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:19,900
Consider, my dear: she is only a child!

279
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:23,200
The Queen turned angrily away from him.

280
00:17:23,800 --> 00:17:25,900
Turn them over!

281
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:28,800
The Knave did so, with one foot.

282
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:31,500
Get up!

283
00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:34,200
The Queen cried in a shrill voice.

284
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:38,200
The three gardeners instantly jumped up, and began bowing to the King, the Queen, and everybody else.

285
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:41,200
Leave off that! You make me giddy.

286
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:43,900
Then, turning to the rose-tree, she went on...

287
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:46,200
What have you been doing here?

288
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:48,900
May it please your Majesty...

289
00:17:49,500 --> 00:17:52,400
Two began, going down on one knee as he spoke.

290
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:54,800
We were trying—

291
00:17:55,400 --> 00:17:57,500
I see!

292
00:17:58,100 --> 00:18:01,200
The Queen, who had been examining the roses, screamed...

293
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:03,800
Off with their heads!

294
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:07,800
The procession moved on, three of the soldiers remaining behind to execute the unfortunate gardeners.

295
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:11,200
They ran to Alice for protection.

296
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:13,900
You shan't be beheaded!

297
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:17,900
She put them into a large flower-pot that stood near.

298
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:21,500
The three soldiers wandered about for a minute or two, looking for them.

299
00:18:22,100 --> 00:18:24,500
Then they quietly marched off after the others.

300
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:27,200
Are their heads off?

301
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:29,900
Their heads are gone, if it please your Majesty!

302
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:32,200
That's right!

303
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:34,800
Can you play croquet?

304
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:37,200
The question was evidently meant for Alice.

305
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:39,500
Yes!

306
00:18:40,100 --> 00:18:41,800
Come on, then!

307
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:45,900
The procession joined in and continued its march.

308
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:49,500
Alice joined the procession, wondering very much what would happen next.

309
00:18:50,100 --> 00:18:52,900
It's—it's a very fine day!

310
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:55,900
A timid voice spoke at her side.

311
00:18:56,500 --> 00:18:59,400
She was walking by the White Rabbit, who was peeping anxiously into her face.

312
00:19:00,100 --> 00:19:01,900
Very. Where's the Duchess?

313
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:04,900
Hush! Hush!

314
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:08,500
The Rabbit looked nervously behind him, and then tipped up on tiptoe.

315
00:19:09,100 --> 00:19:12,400
He put his mouth close to her ear, and whispered...

316
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,500
She's under sentence of execution.

317
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:17,900
What for?

318
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:20,500
Did you say "What a pity!"?

319
00:19:21,100 --> 00:19:22,800
No, I didn't.

320
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:25,900
I don't think it's at all a pity. I said "What for?"

321
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:28,500
She boxed the Queen's ears—

322
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,400
Alice gave a little scream of laughter.

323
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,200
Oh, hush!

324
00:19:35,800 --> 00:19:38,900
The Rabbit whispered in a frightened tone.

325
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:41,900
The Queen will hear you! You see, she came rather late...

326
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:45,900
And the Queen said— Get to your places!

327
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:49,500
The Queen shouted in a voice of thunder, and people began running about in all directions.

328
00:19:50,100 --> 00:19:52,900
Tumbling up against each other.

329
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:56,500
However, they got settled down in a minute or two.

330
00:19:57,100 --> 00:19:59,900
And the game began immediately.

331
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:24,500
Alice thought she had never seen such a curious croquet-ground in all her life.
It was all ridges and furrows.

332
00:20:25,100 --> 00:20:48,900
The balls were live hedgehogs, and the mallets live flamingoes.
The soldiers had to double themselves up and to stand on their hands and feet, to make the arches.

333
00:20:49,400 --> 00:21:02,500
The chief difficulty Alice found at first was in managing her flamingo.
She succeeded in getting its body tucked away, comfortably enough, under her arm.

334
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:18,900
Its legs were hanging down, but generally, just as she had got its neck nicely straightened out...
And was going to give the hedgehog a blow with its head...

335
00:21:19,400 --> 00:21:32,800
It WOULD twist itself round and look up into her face.
With such a puzzled expression that she could not help bursting out laughing.

336
00:21:33,400 --> 00:21:49,500
And when she had got its head down, and was going to begin again...
It was very provoking to find that the hedgehog had unrolled itself.

337
00:21:50,100 --> 00:22:04,200
And was in the act of crawling away.
Besides all this, there was generally a ridge or a furrow in the way wherever she wanted to send the hedgehog.

338
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:19,500
And as the doubled-up soldiers were always getting up and walking to other parts of the ground...
Alice soon came to the conclusion that it was a very difficult game indeed.

339
00:22:20,100 --> 00:22:34,800
The players all played at once without waiting for turns, quarrelling all the while, and fighting for the hedgehogs.
The Queen was in a furious passion, and went stamping about, shouting "Off with his head!" or "Off with her head!" about once in a minute.

340
00:22:35,400 --> 00:22:42,200
Alice began to feel very uneasy.

341
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:58,900
To be sure, she had not as yet had any dispute with the Queen.
But she knew that it might happen any minute.

342
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:12,500
*And then, what would become of me? They're dreadfully fond of beheading people here.*
*The great wonder is, that there's any one left alive!*

343
00:23:13,100 --> 00:23:24,200
She was looking about for some way of escape.
Wondering whether she could get away without being seen...

344
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:38,900
When she noticed a curious appearance in the air.
It puzzled her very much at first, but, after watching it a minute or two, she made it out to be a grin.

345
00:23:39,400 --> 00:23:42,200
It's the Cheshire Cat!

346
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:45,900
Now I shall have somebody to talk to.

347
00:23:46,400 --> 00:23:48,500
How are you getting on?

348
00:23:49,100 --> 00:23:51,900
The Cat waited until more of it appeared before speaking.

349
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:55,200
Alice waited till the eyes appeared, and then nodded.

350
00:23:55,800 --> 00:23:58,900
*It's no use speaking to it, till its ears have come out, or at least one of them.*

351
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:12,500
In another minute the whole head appeared.
Alice put down her flamingo, and began an account of the game.

352
00:24:13,100 --> 00:24:28,200
Feeling very glad she had some one to listen to her.
The Cat seemed to think that there was enough of it now in sight, and no more of it appeared.

353
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:42,900
I don't think they play at all fairly.
And they all quarrel so dreadfully one can't hear oneself speak.

354
00:24:43,400 --> 00:24:58,500
And they don't seem to have any rules in particular.
At least, if there are, nobody attends to them.

355
00:24:59,100 --> 00:25:12,200
And you've no idea how confusing it is all the things being alive.
For instance, there's the arch I've got to go through next walking about at the other end of the ground.

356
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:28,900
And I should have croqueted the Queen's hedgehog just now.
But it ran away when it saw mine coming!

357
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:32,200
How do you like the Queen?

358
00:25:32,800 --> 00:25:35,900
Not at all. She's so extremely—

359
00:25:36,400 --> 00:25:39,900
Alice noticed that the King was standing close behind her, listening.

360
00:25:40,500 --> 00:25:43,400
...likely to win, that it's hardly worth while finishing the game.

361
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:46,900
The King smiled and walked past.

362
00:25:50,500 --> 00:26:12,200
The game drew closer to its wild end.
Arches collapsed and players scattered across the ridges.

363
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:34,900
Alice gathered her flamingo once more, looking back at the fading grin in the air.
The match was over, and the twilight began to fall over the cards.

364
00:26:35,400 --> 00:27:58,500
(shuffling cards) The procession reformed, marching slowly back toward the palace doors.
Alice followed behind, ready for whatever trial or feast awaited them inside.

365
00:28:01,500 --> 00:28:15,000
♪ The Queen of Hearts, she made some tarts, ♪
♪ All on a summer day; ♪

366
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:30,000
♪ The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts, ♪
♪ And took them clean away! ♪

367
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:45,000
♪ Will you walk a little faster? ♪
♪ Said a whiting to a snail. ♪

368
00:28:45,600 --> 00:28:58,000
♪ There's a porpoise close behind us, ♪
♪ And he's treading on my tail. ♪

369
00:28:58,600 --> 00:29:12,000
♪ See how eagerly the lobsters ♪
♪ And the turtles all advance! ♪

370
00:29:12,600 --> 00:29:26,000
♪ They are waiting on the shingle— ♪
♪ Will you come and join the dance? ♪

371
00:29:26,600 --> 00:29:40,000
♪ Will you, won't you, will you, ♪
♪ Won't you, will you join the dance? ♪

372
00:29:40,600 --> 00:29:58,500
♪ Will you, won't you, will you, ♪
♪ Won't you, won't you join the dance? ♪